  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Strong ridging remains centered over the area today \nassociated with a 1035mb high centered over the Canadian Maritimes. \nTemperatures have warmed nicely under clear skies as a result this \nafternoon into the low to mid 60s for many locations. Another clear \nnight is in store with temperatures lowering into the mid 30s to low \n40s. Some cooler air may be possible in the Adirondacks and northern \nSt. Lawrence Valley as winds turn to the northeast beyond midnight, \nleading to cold air drainage from Canada. A weak moisture starved \ncold front will slide south from Canada late tonight into Tuesday \nand wash out over the area with no precipitation expected, though \nsome morning clouds and cooler temperatures can be expected Tuesday \nmorning. Sunny skies will prevail thereafter with large scale \nridging continuing with low humidity and light winds through late \nweek. While prevailing confidence remains high for no precipitation \nthrough late week, there are some signs of light sprinkles across \nthe spine of the Greens late Thursday as another moisture starved \ncold front moves through. While GEFS ensembles show 0% of any \nmeasurable precipitation, the Euro ensembles have been fairly stable \ndenoting a 60% chance for at least some measurable rain along the \nwestern slopes of the Greens Thursday afternoon. Have introduced a \nsmall area of slight chance PoPs (20% or less) for the \naforementioned areas. Given the stout dry layer we will have, \nshowers should they develop, likely would be virga moreover, keeping \nconfidence on the high side for little to no precipitation.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A classic stretch of fall weather will continue late \nthis week and through the weekend. Surface high pressure will build \nsouth out of Quebec for Friday, bringing cold air advection. A brief \nflurry is possible in the mountains early Friday behind the cold \nfront but moisture will be significantly lacking. Highs will drop \ninto the upper 40s to around 50 on Friday. The high should be \ncentered almost directly overhead over the weekend, leading to clear \nskies, light winds and highs in the 50s. Most mornings will be \nfrosty during this stretch, with lows in the 20s and 30s.",
